-- A fixity annotation must sit on the line(s) directly above a binary
-- operator definition. When it lands anywhere else — above a directive or
-- import, inline on a parameter, inside a WHERE body, or stacked above an
-- earlier annotation — it is reported as misplaced and ignored, and crucially
-- it does NOT leak to a later, unrelated definition (which would silently
-- change that operator's meaning).

-- Stranded above a directive: warned, and does not reach `plusB` below.
@infixl 6
#EVAL plusA 1 2

GIVEN p IS A NUMBER
      q IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
p plusA q MEANS p + q

GIVEN p IS A NUMBER
      q IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
p plusB q MEANS p + q

-- plusB never received the stray @infixl 6, so a bare chain over it is still
-- the usual arity situation — but written parenthesized here it evaluates,
-- proving plusB is an ordinary undeclared operator.
#EVAL plusB (plusB 1 2) 3

-- Stacked: the earlier annotation is superseded (warned); the closest one
-- (@infixr 5) wins.
@infixl 9
@infixr 5
GIVEN p IS A NUMBER
      q IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
p consB q MEANS p + 10 * q

-- right-associative: 1 consB (2 consB 3) = 1 + 10*(2+30) = 321
#EVAL consB consB 1 2 consB 3
